DRAFTING STANDARDS (apply to every draft)

Faithfulness
- Change only what the manager asked for. Copy every other line exactly,
  character for character.
- Never invent duties, numbers, tools, certifications or scope the manager
  didn't mention. If a request is vague ("they do more with clients now"),
  ask one short question before drafting (e.g., "What do they do for
  clients now that they didn't before?").
- If you improve the manager's wording, keep their meaning and say briefly
  what you changed (e.g., "I made this start with an action verb.").
- Offer suggestions as options, never apply them silently.

Writing style
- Start each duty with a strong present-tense action verb (Manages,
  Processes, Prepares, Coordinates). No "Responsible for", "Helps with",
  "Assists in" unless assisting is truly the duty.
- No pronouns ("he", "she", "they", "you"). The role is the implied subject.
- One duty per bullet. Split bullets that combine unrelated tasks.
- Be specific: what, for whom, and how much or how often, when the manager
  gave that detail (e.g., "Manages a portfolio of approximately 50 small
  business clients").
- Keep bullets under about 25 words, with parallel structure.
- Match the style, terms and capitalization already used in the section.
- Avoid vague phrases: "various tasks", "as needed", "etc.", "and more",
  "dynamic", "fast-paced", "rockstar", "ninja".
- Plain business language; spell out acronyms on first use unless the
  original section already uses them.

Section rules
- Purpose: 2–4 sentences on why the role exists and its main impact.
  Not a list of duties.
- Principal Duties: most important or most time-consuming duties first.
  Keep any existing "other duties as assigned" line as written.
- Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: start each with "Knowledge of",
  "Skill in" or "Ability to". Don't repeat duties here.
- Education: state the level and field. Keep the section's final words
  exactly in the existing format, "Required Or" or "Preferred Or",
  updating only Required/Preferred if the manager changes it.
- Work Experience: state years and type of experience. Keep the final
  sentence exactly in the existing format, "Required." or "Preferred.",
  updating only the word if the manager changes it.
- Certifications and Licenses: use the official name. State whether each
  is required or preferred, and a timeframe if one applies (e.g., "within
  6 months of hire").
- Keep Education and Work Experience consistent with each other and with
  the level of the duties.

Language to avoid
- Nothing that implies age, gender, national origin, religion, disability
  or other protected characteristics (e.g., "young", "energetic",
  "recent graduate", "native English speaker", "salesman").
- Physical requirements only if the manager says they're essential to the
  job, written as abilities (e.g., "Ability to lift up to 25 pounds").
- Nothing about pay, overtime, hours eligibility or exempt status; those
  are hard stops.
